batch_1_sections = {
    # PART III
    "Designation of professions": "(1) The built environment professions regulated under this Act shall include architecture, quantity surveying, engineering, physical planning, landscape architecture, construction management, interior design, and any other profession designated by the Cabinet Secretary.\n(2) The Cabinet Secretary may, by notice in the Gazette and upon the recommendation of the Council, designate additional built environment professions subject to the criteria set out in section 38.",
    "Recognition of existing statutory professional regulators": "(1) The existing statutory bodies established prior to the commencement of this Act to regulate specific built environment professions, including the Board of Registration of Architects and Quantity Surveyors (BORAQS) and the Engineers Board of Kenya (EBK), are hereby recognized as constituent professional regulators under the umbrella of the Council.\n(2) These constituent bodies shall retain their day-to-day administrative functions but shall harmonize their standards, fee structures, and disciplinary frameworks with the overriding policies established by the Council.",
    "Profession-specific regulatory autonomy": "(1) Subject to the overriding coordination mandate of the Council, constituent professional regulators shall retain autonomy over the specific technical standards, peer review mechanisms, and specialized continuous professional development pertinent to their respective disciplines.\n(2) Any technical standard or regulation proposed by a constituent regulator must be submitted to the Council for ratification to ensure it does not conflict with cross-disciplinary safety mandates.",
    "Recognition of new professions": "(1) A group of practitioners seeking recognition as a distinct built environment profession may petition the Council for statutory recognition.\n(2) The Council shall evaluate the petition based on the distinctiveness of the body of knowledge, the risk profile of the work, and the public interest served by regulating the proposed profession.\n(3) If satisfied, the Council shall recommend the designation of the new profession to the Cabinet Secretary.",
    "Relationship between umbrella Council and profession regulators": "(1) The Council shall act as the apex regulatory and appellate body for the built environment sector.\n(2) Where a conflict arises between a directive of the Council and a regulation of a constituent professional regulator, the directive of the Council shall prevail to the extent necessary to protect public safety and ensure inter-disciplinary harmony.",

    # PART IV
    "Student registration": "(1) A person enrolled in an accredited academic program in a recognized university or tertiary institution may apply to the Council for registration as a student.\n(2) A registered student may undertake practical training under the direct supervision of a registered professional but shall not independently undertake reserved professional work or assume statutory liability.",
    "Graduate/Candidate registration": "(1) A person who has successfully completed an accredited academic program but has not yet completed the requisite practical experience and professional examinations shall be registered as a graduate or candidate.\n(2) A graduate shall practice only under the continuous supervision of a registered professional of the same discipline.",
    "Technician registration": "(1) A person holding an accredited diploma or equivalent qualification in a built environment discipline may apply for registration as a technician.\n(2) A registered technician may independently execute specific scopes of work designated as low-risk or strictly procedural by the Council, subject to the competency framework in Part VIII.",
    "Technologist registration": "(1) A person holding an accredited higher diploma or a Bachelor of Technology degree in a built environment discipline may apply for registration as a technologist.\n(2) A registered technologist may independently execute specific scopes of work designated as medium-risk, or complex work under the supervision of a registered professional.",
    "Professional registration": "(1) A person holding an accredited bachelor's or master's degree in a built environment discipline, who has completed the statutory period of practical experience and passed the professional examinations, shall be registered as a professional.\n(2) A registered professional may independently undertake reserved professional work, assume full statutory liability, and act as the principal consultant on complex and high-risk projects.",
    "Specialist registration": "(1) A registered professional who has acquired advanced academic qualifications and demonstrable expertise in a specific sub-discipline (such as structural engineering, acoustic design, or sustainable architecture) may apply for specialist registration.\n(2) The Council shall maintain a separate register of specialists and may mandate that certain ultra-high-risk projects be undertaken only by registered specialists.",
    "Temporary and foreign registration": "(1) A foreign national possessing qualifications equivalent to those required under this Act may apply for temporary registration to undertake a specific, specialized project in Kenya.\n(2) Temporary registration shall be valid only for the duration of the specified project and shall be contingent upon the foreign practitioner entering into a joint venture with a local registered firm, ensuring skills transfer.",
    "Mutual recognition agreements": "(1) The Council may, with the approval of the Cabinet Secretary, enter into Mutual Recognition Agreements (MRAs) with equivalent regulatory authorities in other jurisdictions.\n(2) The Council shall establish a dedicated framework to operationalize MRAs under the East African Community Common Market Protocol and the African Continental Free Trade Area (AfCFTA) Agreement, facilitating the cross-border practice of Kenyan professionals.\n(3) An MRA shall facilitate the reciprocal registration of professionals, provided the Council is satisfied that the regulatory standards of the foreign jurisdiction are substantially equivalent to those established under this Act.",
    "Regional integration": "In executing its mandate regarding foreign registration, the Council shall give effect to the provisions of the East African Community Common Market Protocol and the African Continental Free Trade Area (AfCFTA) Agreement concerning the free movement of professional services.",
    "Competency-based registration": "(1) All applications for registration, regardless of category, shall be evaluated on a competency-based framework rather than strictly on duration of experience.\n(2) The Council shall publish specific competency matrices detailing the technical, managerial, and ethical outcomes an applicant must demonstrate prior to registration.",
    "Education and qualification requirements": "(1) The Council shall publish a schedule of accredited academic qualifications that satisfy the educational requirements for each registration category.\n(2) No person shall be registered unless they hold a qualification formally accredited by the Council or a recognized constituent regulator.",
    "Practical experience": "(1) An applicant for professional registration must complete a statutory period of structured practical experience, typically not less than twenty-four months, under the supervision of a registered professional.\n(2) The supervisor must certify the candidate's logbook and declare that the candidate has attained the necessary practical competencies.",
    "Professional examinations": "(1) The Council, directly or through its constituent regulators, shall administer professional practice examinations at least twice annually.\n(2) The examinations shall test the candidate's knowledge of professional ethics, statutory laws, contract administration, and technical competence.",
    "Recognition of prior learning": "(1) The Council shall establish a framework for the Recognition of Prior Learning (RPL) to allow highly experienced practitioners who lack formal academic qualifications to be assessed and registered in appropriate categories.\n(2) RPL assessments shall be rigorous and must prioritize public safety and verifiable technical competence.",
    "Recognition of foreign qualifications": "(1) A person holding a foreign academic qualification may apply to the Council for its recognition.\n(2) The Council shall assess the foreign qualification against the Kenyan competency standards and may require the applicant to undertake remedial coursework or bridging examinations before granting recognition.",
    "Application for registration": "(1) An application for registration shall be made to the Registrar in the prescribed form, accompanied by the prescribed fee and certified copies of academic certificates, logbooks, and identification documents.\n(2) The Registrar shall verify the authenticity of all submitted documents and forward the application to the relevant Registration Committee.",
    "Registration decisions": "(1) The Registration Committee shall consider the application and may approve the registration, reject the application, or require the applicant to fulfill additional conditions.\n(2) Where an application is rejected, the Committee shall provide the applicant with written reasons for the rejection within fourteen days.",
    "Suspension and cancellation of registration": "(1) The Council may suspend or cancel the registration of any person who is convicted of a serious criminal offence, is found guilty of gross professional misconduct under Part XI, or obtained registration through fraud or misrepresentation.\n(2) The name of a person whose registration is cancelled shall be struck off the register and published in the Gazette.",
    "Restoration of registration": "(1) A person whose registration has been cancelled may, after the expiration of a period specified by the Disciplinary Committee, apply to the Council for restoration to the register.\n(2) The Council may restore the registration subject to such conditions as it deems fit, including mandatory retraining or supervised practice.",

    # PART V
    "Establishment of national professional register": "(1) The Registrar shall establish and maintain a National Register of Built Environment Professionals, which shall be kept in electronic format.\n(2) The Register shall be subdivided into the distinct professional categories and disciplines recognized under this Act.",
    "Information contained in register": "The Register shall contain, in respect of every registered person: their full name, physical and postal address, registration number, category of registration, date of registration, details of any disciplinary sanctions, and the status of their current practising certificate.",
    "Public access": "(1) The National Register shall be a public document.\n(2) The Council shall ensure that the Register is accessible to the public free of charge via a secure online portal, allowing any person to verify the registration status of a professional in real-time.",
    "National built environment project database": "(1) The Council shall establish and maintain a National Built Environment Project Database.\n(2) Every county government and national procuring entity shall transmit metadata of all approved built environment projects to this database within seven days of approval.",
    "Golden thread of information": "(1) The developer, principal designer, and principal contractor of any high-risk building shall have a statutory duty to create, maintain, and securely store a digital 'Golden Thread' of information across the entire lifecycle of the building.\n(2) The Golden Thread shall contain a continuously updated, auditable trail of all design decisions, material specifications, and structural alterations, ensuring that subsequent owners and regulators can trace the origin of any safety-critical component.",
    "Linking professionals to projects": "(1) The project database shall strictly link the registration number of the principal professionals (architect, engineers, quantity surveyor, etc.) to every approved project.\n(2) This linkage shall be used to track professional liability, prevent the unauthorized affixation of professional seals, and monitor the workload capacity of individual professionals.",
    "Digital verification": "(1) The Council shall implement a digital verification system, such as QR codes or cryptographic signatures, to be affixed to all professional drawings, reports, and certificates.\n(2) County governments shall not approve any development application that lacks verifiable digital signatures from registered professionals.",
    "Digital certificates": "The Council may issue digital registration certificates and practising certificates, which shall carry the same legal weight as physical certificates and shall be cryptographically verifiable.",
    "Data protection": "(1) The Council shall be designated as a Data Controller for the purposes of the Data Protection Act, 2019.\n(2) In maintaining the Register and the Project Database, the Council shall strictly comply with the Data Protection Act, ensuring the security, integrity, and privacy of personal and commercially sensitive data.\n(3) The Council must establish explicit data governance guidelines detailing what professional information is published on the public portal and what remains strictly confidential.",
    "Correction of information": "A registered person shall notify the Registrar of any change in their registered particulars within thirty days of such change, and the Registrar shall promptly amend the Register accordingly.",
    "Audit trails": "The digital infrastructure maintaining the Register and Project Database shall incorporate immutable audit trails to record all alterations, additions, or deletions of data, ensuring accountability and preventing tampering.",

    # PART VI
    "Requirement for practising certificate": "(1) A registered person shall not practice or offer professional services unless they hold a valid practising certificate issued by the Council.\n(2) A registration certificate alone does not authorize a person to practice; it merely denotes their inclusion in the Register.",
    "Application": "An application for a practising certificate shall be made annually to the Registrar in the prescribed form and accompanied by the prescribed fee.",
    "Conditions": "The Council shall issue a practising certificate only if the applicant is in good standing, has complied with the mandatory Continuing Professional Development (CPD) requirements, and holds valid professional indemnity insurance.",
    "Validity": "A practising certificate shall be valid from the date of issue until the thirty-first day of December of the year in which it is issued, unless suspended or cancelled earlier under this Act.",
    "Renewal": "A professional must apply for the renewal of their practising certificate at least thirty days before its expiration to ensure continuous authorization to practice.",
    "Continuing professional development": "(1) The Council shall establish a mandatory Continuing Professional Development (CPD) framework to ensure professionals maintain and enhance their technical knowledge and ethical standards.\n(2) The Council shall accredit specific courses, seminars, and activities for CPD points.",
    "CPD compliance": "(1) Every registered professional must accumulate a prescribed minimum number of CPD points annually to qualify for the renewal of their practising certificate.\n(2) The Council may grant exemptions from CPD requirements on grounds of severe illness, extended maternity leave, or other extenuating circumstances.",
    "Professional indemnity insurance": "(1) Every professional offering independent services to the public must maintain a valid professional indemnity insurance policy to cover potential liabilities arising from professional negligence or errors.\n(2) The minimum limit of indemnity shall be prescribed by the Council based on the risk profile of the professional's scope of work.",
    "Run-off insurance cover": "(1) A professional or firm ceasing to practice, whether due to retirement, dissolution, or bankruptcy, must procure and maintain 'run-off' professional indemnity insurance cover.\n(2) The run-off cover must remain active for a minimum period of ten years following the cessation of practice to guarantee the enforceability of decennial liability claims.",
    "Suspension and non-renewal": "The Council shall refuse to renew, or may suspend, a practising certificate if the holder fails to meet CPD requirements, fails to maintain professional indemnity insurance, or defaults on the payment of statutory fees.",

    # PART VII
    "Requirement for firm registration": "(1) No corporate entity, partnership, or sole proprietorship shall offer built environment professional services to the public unless it is registered as a Professional Firm by the Council.\n(2) Operating an unregistered firm constitutes a severe offence punishable under Part XIV.",
    "Eligibility of firms": "A firm shall be eligible for registration if it is incorporated or registered under the laws of Kenya, and if a prescribed minimum percentage of its shares or partnership interests are beneficially held by registered professionals.",
    "Responsible professional": "(1) Every registered firm must designate at least one registered professional as the 'Responsible Professional' who shall be a director or principal partner.\n(2) The Responsible Professional shall be personally accountable for the technical and ethical standards of the firm's output, alongside the corporate liability of the firm.",
    "Multidisciplinary firms": "(1) The Council shall permit and regulate the registration of multidisciplinary firms that offer a combination of built environment services (e.g., architecture, engineering, and quantity surveying).\n(2) A multidisciplinary firm must have a designated Responsible Professional for each distinct discipline it offers.",
    "Foreign firms": "(1) A foreign firm may be registered to undertake a specific project in Kenya if it proves specialized expertise not readily available locally.\n(2) Registration of a foreign firm shall be conditional upon forming a joint venture with a local registered firm and submitting a binding skills-transfer plan.",
    "Joint ventures": "Local registered firms may form joint ventures to undertake large-scale projects. The joint venture itself must notify the Council of its composition and designate the primary Responsible Professionals for the venture.",
    "Professional independence": "A registered firm shall structure its operations to ensure that the professional judgment of its registered staff is not compromised by commercial pressures, non-professional directors, or external shareholders.",
    "Quality management": "The Council may require registered firms to implement and maintain recognized Quality Management Systems (QMS) as a condition for the renewal of their firm registration.",
    "Professional records": "Every registered firm shall maintain secure and comprehensive records of all projects undertaken, including design calculations, site meeting minutes, and financial records, for a minimum period of ten years from project completion.",
    "Professional indemnity insurance": "A registered firm must maintain a corporate professional indemnity insurance policy commensurate with the scale, complexity, and aggregated risk of the projects it undertakes."
}
